From 151a5b8149697e5965eadde64e58460e06e8d7c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aquarat <3090997+aquarat@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 15:39:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] thunderbolt: apple: Enable E2E flow control on Tx rings
 too

End-to-end flow control on the Apple NHI only works when it is enabled on
both rings of a channel: the Rx ring returns credits to the remote end,
and the remote Tx ring only waits for them when its own E2E bit is set.
With E2E enabled on the Rx ring alone, which is what the NHI core and
thunderbolt-net program, the receiving ring is programmed correctly
(options 0x90001000: valid, E2E, Tx HopID 1) but the sending Tx ring
(0x80000000) never stalls on credits. Once the receiver falls behind it
runs out of descriptors and drops frames; thunderbolt-net counts them as
rx_missed_errors and TCP retransmits. Turning the e2e module parameter of
thunderbolt-net off makes no difference.

Add a tx_e2e flag to struct tb_nhi for host interfaces that need
RING_FLAG_E2E on the Tx ring as well, and set it for the Apple NHI.
Users of E2E flow control can then enable it on their Tx ring too. Hosts
that do not set the flag keep the current behaviour: the Falcon Ridge
E2E workaround, for example, sends its Rx credits to an unused HopID, so
a Tx ring that waited for them would stall.

The new member sits in the padding after iommu_dma_protection, so the
layout of struct tb_nhi does not change.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: aquarat <3090997+aquarat@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c | 5 +++++
 include/linux/thunderbolt.h | 4 ++++
 2 files changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c
index 530008cc8..1e6d2e583 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c
@@ -531,6 +531,11 @@ static int apple_nhi_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 
 	spin_lock_init(&anhi->nhi.lock);
 	anhi->nhi.iommu_dma_protection = true;
+	/*
+	 * A Tx ring ignores the credits returned by the remote Rx ring unless
+	 * E2E flow control is enabled on the Tx ring too, as macOS does.
+	 */
+	anhi->nhi.tx_e2e = true;
 	anhi->nhi.ops = &apple_nhi_ops;
 	anhi->nhi.ring_layout = &apple_nhi_ring_layout;
 	anhi->nhi.iobase = anhi->nhi_base;
diff --git a/include/linux/thunderbolt.h b/include/linux/thunderbolt.h
index 840115962..ce58caf40 100644
--- a/include/linux/thunderbolt.h
+++ b/include/linux/thunderbolt.h
@@ -511,6 +511,9 @@ void tb_service_properties_changed(struct tb_service *svc);
  * @going_away: The host controller device is about to disappear so when
  *		this flag is set, avoid touching the hardware anymore.
  * @iommu_dma_protection: An IOMMU will isolate external-facing ports.
+ * @tx_e2e: Tx rings only wait for end-to-end flow control credits when
+ *	    %RING_FLAG_E2E is set on them as well. Without it the sender
+ *	    ignores the credits and overruns the receiving ring.
  * @interrupt_work: Work scheduled to handle ring interrupt when no
  *		    MSI-X is used.
  * @hop_count: Number of rings (end point hops) supported by NHI.
@@ -527,6 +530,7 @@ struct tb_nhi {
 	struct tb_ring **rx_rings;
 	bool going_away;
 	bool iommu_dma_protection;
+	bool tx_e2e;
 	struct work_struct interrupt_work;
 	u32 hop_count;
 	unsigned long quirks;
